{# -------------------------------------------------------------------- FAQ --- #}
<section class="shell" style="padding-bottom:clamp(3rem,8vh,5rem); max-width:min(var(--shell-max), 60rem);">
  <h2 class="display-md">Questions</h2>
  <div style="margin-top:2rem;">

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-trigger">
        <span>Why no JavaScript?</span>
        <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-panel">
        Because none is needed. Text, images and mathematics are all things HTML
        and CSS do well. The three scripts that do ship — a lightbox, a search
        field and a reading-width control — are conveniences, and each is hidden
        entirely when scripting is unavailable rather than left visible and dead.
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-trigger">
        <span>Can I reuse the page blocks?</span>
        <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-panel">
        Yes. <a class="link-underline" href="/block_test_page.html">block_test_page.html</a>
        holds every block type this site knows how to render, each fenced with
        copy-from / copy-to comments. Paste one into a file in
        <code>input_custom_html/</code>, add front matter, and rebuild.
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-trigger">
        <span>How are images handled?</span>
        <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-panel">
        Every photograph has a compressed counterpart under
        <code>image_min/</code>, and that is what a page loads. The full
        resolution file is only fetched when you open the lightbox. If a
        counterpart is missing the build generates one and says so.
      </div>
    </details>

  </div>
</section>
